  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Tennyson was Poet Laureate for forty-two years and the most public poet of the Victorian age.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It was written in 1889, when Tennyson was eighty, on a crossing of the Solent after an illness.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He asked that it close every collection of his poems, which makes it a designed last word.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sixteen lines in four groups of four.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Line one rhymes with line three and line two with line four — star and bar, me and sea.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The lines vary in length, with the fourth line of each quatrain usually the shortest — which is what gives each stanza its dying fall.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A bar is crossed on a full tide, which is why stanza two prays for a tide deep enough to carry the keel over.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The crossing of the bar is the moment of dying, and the poem's whole metaphor depends on that identification.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The opening sets the hour and the summons: sunset, the star, and one clear call.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The call is *clear* — the speaker knows what is happening and that it is for him.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "And may there be no moaning of the bar" — the sea must not sound like grief at the crossing.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fullness without noise is the poem's ideal death: complete, and quiet.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The bar is given a human sound, which is exactly what the prayer asks to be spared.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tide, the sound and the foam are all details of one request: let the crossing be quiet.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line is theological as well as nautical: what came from the deep returns to it, which is the poem's account of death.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The second time-marker moves the poem from sunset to twilight, and the light continues to fail.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The exclamation mark after "dark" is the one moment of shock in an otherwise calm poem.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "And may there be no sadness of farewell, / When I embark" — the prayer is for those he leaves, not only for himself.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Bourne" is an old word for a boundary or destination; time and place are the shore the speaker is leaving.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line concedes distance and does not resist it, which is part of the poem's acceptance.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The capital letter and the phrase "face to face" identify God; a pilot is the one who knows the water and brings the ship in.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Paul writes that now we see through a glass darkly, but then face to face; the poem borrows the hope without quoting the chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The last line is a temporal clause — when I have crossed the bar — and the meeting with the Pilot is the hope that fills it.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nothing is accused and nothing is feared; the speaker agrees to the crossing and hopes for a meeting.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The whole poem is about dying and never names it, which is how the extended metaphor does its work.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The harbour, the bar, the tide, the pilot and the crossing all belong to one system, and the system is the argument.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The optative "may" is the grammar of prayer, and it recurs at the same point in two stanzas.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each stanza ends on a shorter line, so the poem falls away four times, like water leaving a shore.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The archaic contraction slows the last line and gives its monosyllables weight; Tennyson is a poet to be heard before he is understood.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sunset, star, twilight, bell, tide, bar, flood, Pilot — the poem's whole vocabulary is one hour and one element.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Even the dangerous place is crossed on a tide too full for sound and foam; the calm is the prayer's content.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A pilot comes aboard because he knows the local water; the poem gives that knowledge to God at the moment of death.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The prayer is for the living as much as for the dying; the speaker asks that the parting not be made harder.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem has no public or political subject at all; it is a personal meditation on dying.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The light fails deliberately across three quatrains, and the fourth answers the darkness with a face.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nautical detail is exact so that the metaphor can carry the weight; mistaking vehicle for tenor misses the whole poem.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A poem placed last in every collection is being given the last word, and the request is part of how the poem means.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    That sentence holds the metaphor, the tone and the hope together, which is what a theme question rewards.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Vanity's dead have crossed and left signs; Tennyson's speaker is about to cross. The shared idea is passage, not extinction.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The first person and the future prospect make it a meditation on the speaker's own dying, which is why the tone is personal rather than public.
  ]

]

